[[:db/retract 17592186175588 :answer-field/choices 17592186175589] [:db/retract 17592186175588 :answer-field/choices 17592186175590] [:db/retract 17592186175588 :answer-field/choices 17592186175592] [:db/retract 17592186175588 :answer-field/choices 17592186175593] {:db/id 17592186175781 :question/problem "Determine whether ![](images/85/8594aa29c1348769c738a5ed407df28d27324d8e6d8679971b3a5825de6aac68.svg) is an eigenvector of the matrix ![](images/81/8111e3afdd5628fe312f17d6632b2d8c5204ddc9e7141e2e167ea560aad55e83.svg)\nIf so, then what is the corresponding eigenvalue?" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186175780 :question/problem "Find an eigenvector of \n![](images/cc/cc6a84d8a040240089d946999190e4d118fee2be70df2958a6a59d6534bcf429.svg)\nthat corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda=-3+4\\textrm{i}.$" :question/worked-solution "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A - \\lambda I) \\textbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\,\\lambda =-3+4\\textrm{i},$ and\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 4 \\\\ -4 & -3\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}-3+4\\text{i} & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3+4\\text{i}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-4\\text{i} & 4 \\\\ -4 & -4\\text{i}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the homogeneous system of linear equations\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}-4\\text{i}{x}_{1}+4{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ -4{x}_{1}-4\\text{i}{x}_{2}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since the matrix $A-(-3+4\\textrm{i})I$ is singular, the system above must have a non-zero solution. This means that one equation of the system is a multiple of another.\n\nAs a result, we can drop one of the rows and take one of the variables to be the free variable. For example, from the second equation, we get\n\n$$\nx_1 = -\\textrm{i}x_2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is given by\n\n![](images/1c/1c7fa984e0d00619ea2e8647f560efc3f928b643875f0cbf973ca8bb2a931e3e.svg)\n\nSetting $x_2=1,$ we get the eigenvector ![](images/ec/ec3f75c25f412d466b7acfc4810ecf6987038a23ee5da0dfdd98cc181e37e0c7.svg)" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186175782 :question/problem "Let ![](images/53/5337ed67f212c0029ae2317c658672aa3f3f68a7614c8ce4dfafb6e92d2f2229.svg) and ![](images/4f/4fad8749e9d06a4cafdd089e6ed982131a8957fff201d865fb9da323c5c27bf5.svg) be linearly independent eigenvectors of the matrix\n![](images/a0/a05c08cb757f62c9b874fce3b3ae9db1d4278f1f96194a3e92df0a4d457b591c.svg)\nGiven that $\\lambda_1=4+5\\textrm{i}$ is an eigenvalue of $A,$ find the value of ${\\dfrac{a_1a_2}{b_1b_2}}.$" :question/worked-solution "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A - \\lambda I) \\textbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\,\\lambda_1 =4+5\\textrm{i}$ and\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-{\\lambda }_{1}I & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & 5 \\\\ -5 & 4\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}4+5\\text{i} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4+5\\text{i}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-5\\text{i} & 5 \\\\ -5 & -5\\text{i}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the homogeneous system of linear equations\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}-5\\text{i}{x}_{1}+5{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ -5{x}_{1}-5\\text{i}{x}_{2}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since the matrix $A-(4+5\\textrm{i})I$ is singular, the system above must have a non-zero solution. This means that one equation of the system is a multiple of another.\n\nAs a result, we can drop one of the rows and take one of the variables as the free variable. For example, from the first equation, we get\n\n$$\nx_2 = \\textrm{i}x_1.\n$$\n\nSo, the general solution is given by\n\n![](images/33/334bab555d470ebcc0d36eb4e2140530d4f92b2fa13e4adbfea848250d92de7b.svg)\n\nSetting $x_1=1,$ we get the eigenvector\n\n![](images/82/82d57d12bd8027fc3c7e9b368b752616ed33ffcce577b956c91cb8a721dd5905.svg) \n\nNow, since $\\lambda_1=4+5\\textrm{i}$ is a complex eigenvalue of $A,$ the second eigenvalue of $A$ must be the complex conjugate of $\\lambda_1.$ Therefore, we have \n\n$$\n\\lambda_2=\\overline{\\lambda_1}=4-5\\textrm{i}.\n$$\n\nMoreover, the corresponding eigenvector must be the complex conjugate of $\\mathbf v_1.$ Therefore, we have\n\n![](images/8c/8cd66a9324ee5cf95277457fc1eef8ddb30d36543b71d4446eb93b5a2d8313a7.svg)\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n{\\dfrac{a_1a_2}{b_1b_2}} = {\\dfrac{(1)(1)}{(\\textrm{i})(-\\textrm{i})}} = 1.\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186176575 :question/problem "Determine whether $v=[\\begin{aligned}\\text{i} \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]$ is an eigenvector of the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ -4 & 2\\end{aligned}].$\nIf so, then what is the corresponding eigenvalue?" :question/worked-solution "The eigenvectors of $A$ are the non-zero vectors $v$ such that $Av=\\lambda v.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Av & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ -4 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}\\text{i} \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2+2\\text{i} \\\\ 4-4\\text{i}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2{\\text{i}}^{2}+2\\text{i} \\\\ 4-4\\text{i}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\text{i}(2-2\\text{i}) \\\\ 2(2-2\\text{i})\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =(2-2\\text{i})[\\begin{aligned}\\text{i} \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =(2-2\\text{i})v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $Av=(2-2\\text{i})v,$ we conclude that $v$ is an eigenvector of $A$ and the corresponding eigenvalue is $\\lambda =2-2\\text{i}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186175588 :answer-field/correct "answer-58a69195-600a-5e46-b79b-827cb7f39b55" :answer-field/choices ["answer-05a52154-a704-5e15-a11d-70a80d71913b" "answer-4f8367f4-f25a-5797-be99-f2deef2ca2c1" "answer-395b71eb-934f-50c8-901b-d659116b2b06" "answer-58a69195-600a-5e46-b79b-827cb7f39b55"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-05a52154-a704-5e15-a11d-70a80d71913b" :answer/id #uuid "05a52154-a704-5e15-a11d-70a80d71913b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\lambda =1+\\text{i}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4f8367f4-f25a-5797-be99-f2deef2ca2c1" :answer/id #uuid "4f8367f4-f25a-5797-be99-f2deef2ca2c1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\lambda =1-\\text{i}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-395b71eb-934f-50c8-901b-d659116b2b06" :answer/id #uuid "395b71eb-934f-50c8-901b-d659116b2b06" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\lambda =2+2\\text{i}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-58a69195-600a-5e46-b79b-827cb7f39b55" :answer/id #uuid "58a69195-600a-5e46-b79b-827cb7f39b55" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\lambda =2-2\\text{i}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186176481 :question/worked-solution "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\,\\lambda =-2\\text{i},$ and\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =[\\begin{aligned}10 & 4 \\\\ -26 & -10\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}-2\\text{i} & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2\\text{i}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}10+2\\text{i} & 4 \\\\ -26 & -10+2\\text{i}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the homogeneous system of linear equations\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}(10+2\\text{i}){x}_{1}+4{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ -26{x}_{1}+(-10+2\\text{i}){x}_{2}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since the matrix $A+2\\text{i}I$ is singular, the system above must have a non-zero solution. This means that one equation of the system is a multiple of another. As a result, we can drop one of the rows and take one of the variables to be the free variable. For example, from the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n{x}_{2}=-\\frac{1}{2}(5+\\text{i}){x}_{1}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{2}(5+\\text{i}){x}_{1}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{1}\\in C.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{2}=-2,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\nv=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 5+\\text{i}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, $\\frac{b}{a}=\\frac{5+\\text{i}}{-2}=-\\frac{5}{2}-\\frac{1}{2}\\text{i}.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186351201 :question/worked-solution "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\,\\lambda =-1+3\\text{i},$ and\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 & -1 \\\\ 13 & 1\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}-1+3\\text{i} & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1+3\\text{i}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2-3\\text{i} & -1 \\\\ 13 & 2-3\\text{i}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the homogeneous system of linear equations\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}(-2-3\\text{i}){x}_{1}-{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ 13{x}_{1}+(2-3\\text{i}){x}_{2}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since the matrix $A-(-1+3\\text{i})I$ is singular, the system above must have a non-zero solution. This means that one equation of the system is a multiple of another. As a result, we can drop one of the rows and take one of the variables to be the free variable. For example, from the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n{x}_{2}=(-2-3\\text{i}){x}_{1}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ -(2+3\\text{i}){x}_{1}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{1}\\in C.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{1}=-1,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\nv=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2+3\\text{i}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, $\\frac{a}{b}=\\frac{-1}{2+3\\text{i}}=\\frac{-(2-3\\text{i})}{13}=-\\frac{2}{13}+\\frac{3}{13}\\text{i}.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186326746 :question/worked-solution "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\,{\\lambda }_{1}=1-3\\text{i}$ and\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-{\\lambda }_{1}I & =[\\begin{aligned}7 & 3 \\\\ -15 & -5\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}1-3\\text{i} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1-3\\text{i}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}6+3\\text{i} & 3 \\\\ -15 & -6+3\\text{i}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the homogeneous system of linear equations\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}(6+3\\text{i}){x}_{1}+3{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ -15{x}_{1}+(-6+3\\text{i}){x}_{2}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since the matrix $A-(1-3\\text{i})I$ is singular, the system above must have a non-zero solution. This means that one equation of the system is a multiple of another. As a result, we can drop one of the rows and take one of the variables as the free variable. For example, from the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=\\frac{1}{5}(-2+\\text{i}){x}_{2}\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{5}(-2+\\text{i}){x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in C.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{2}=5,$ we get the eigenvector\n\n$$\n{\\text{v}}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-2+\\text{i} \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n \n\nOn the other hand, since ${\\lambda }_{1}=1-3\\text{i}$ is a complex eigenvalue of $A,$ the second eigenvalue of $A$ must be the  complex conjugate of ${\\lambda }_{1}.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{2}=\\overset{―}{{\\lambda }_{1}}=1+3\\text{i}.\n$$\n\nMoreover, the corresponding eigenvector to ${\\lambda }_{2}$ is\n\n$$\n{v}_{2}={\\overset{―}{v}}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-2-\\text{i} \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\frac{{b}_{1}{b}_{2}}{{a}_{1}{a}_{2}}=\\frac{(5)(5)}{(-2+\\text{i})(-2-\\text{i})}=5.\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186272076 :question/worked-solution "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\,{\\lambda }_{1}=1+5\\text{i}$ and\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-{\\lambda }_{1}I & =[\\begin{aligned}-4 & -5 \\\\ 10 & 6\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}1+5\\text{i} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1+5\\text{i}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-5-5\\text{i} & -5 \\\\ 10 & 5-5\\text{i}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the homogeneous system of linear equations\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}(-5-5\\text{i}){x}_{1}-5{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ 10{x}_{1}+(5-5\\text{i}){x}_{2}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since the matrix $A-(1+5\\text{i})I$ is singular, the system above must have a non-zero solution. This means that one equation of the system is a multiple of another. As a result, we can drop one of the rows and take one of the variables as the free variable. For example, from the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=-\\frac{1}{2}(1-\\text{i}){x}_{2}\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{2}(1-\\text{i}){x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in C.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{2}=-2,$ we get the eigenvector\n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1-\\text{i} \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, since ${\\lambda }_{1}=1+5\\text{i}$ is a complex eigenvalue of $A,$ the second eigenvalue of $A$ must be the complex conjugate of ${\\lambda }_{1}.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{2}=\\overset{―}{{\\lambda }_{1}}=1-5\\text{i}.\n$$\n\nMoreover, the corresponding eigenvector to ${\\lambda }_{2}$ is\n\n$$\n{v}_{2}={\\overset{―}{v}}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1+\\text{i} \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\frac{{a}_{1}{a}_{2}}{{b}_{1}{b}_{2}}=\\frac{(1-\\text{i})(1+\\text{i})}{(-2)(-2)}=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186176975 :tutorial/content "Let's find the eigenvector corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda=-\\textrm{i}$ of the matrix ![](images/a3/a351d711bda1d0b7009e0c2aa9ac8265e061e103f24dc2ff8c221711189a57e7.svg)\n\nTo compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A - \\lambda I) \\textbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\,\\lambda =-\\textrm{i},$ and\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}-\\text{i} & 0 \\\\ 0 & -\\text{i}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1+\\text{i} & -1 \\\\ 2 & -1+\\text{i}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the homogeneous system of linear equations\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}(1+\\text{i}){x}_{1}-{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ 2{x}_{1}+(-1+\\text{i}){x}_{2}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since the matrix $A-(-\\textrm{i})I$ is singular, the system above must have a non-zero solution. This means that one equation of the system is a multiple of another.\n\nAs a result, we can drop one of the rows and take one of the variables as the free variable. For example, from the first equation, we get\n\n$$\nx_2 = (1+\\textrm{i})x_1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by setting $x_1\\in\\mathbb C$ to be the free variable, we find that the general solution to our system is given by\n\n![](images/8e/8eef5a23053809341f427e7099c364d65fa24d8144e4feac2a9d817accaa5a88.svg)\n\nSetting $x_1=1,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n![](images/40/40c4333ba45ec2afa3479f682fcba72c2b61d69c4a4611b30b0d4078731e0a8b.svg)\n\nNotice that our eigenvector has complex-valued entries.\n\n**Note:** To check our result, we can show that $A\\mathbf v = \\lambda \\mathbf v.$ Computing $A\\mathbf v,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Av & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1+\\text{i}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-\\text{i} \\\\ 1-\\text{i}\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand computing $\\lambda \\mathbf v$ gives\n\n![](images/12/12fed2087f821e6e5da14d844b61e8300c8a4dc98ac4eff96f719fcc5795b5b4.svg)\n\nSo $A\\mathbf v = \\lambda \\mathbf v,$ as required." :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186176123 :activity/expected-seconds 960.0 :db/ensure [:activity/validate]}]
